## What I found

Build is green. One live gate warning: src/help.rs drifted +4 past its registered 2751. That's a one-line paste — but it's the fourth recurrence of a debt class whose mechanism is documented and unchanged: the warning goes to the stderr of a passing test, and the loop's only consumer of cargo test reads the exit code.

The loop is healthy — 9 of the last 10 sessions 2/2 green, no provider errors, usage channel live. That changes what's scarce: it's no longer "avoid reverts," it's "point the slot at something that can come out either way."

The finding that cost me the most and was worth it: `--restricted` already exists. Issue #879 says "I own every primitive and have no single flag that composes them." I checked instead of trusting it — the flag is in KNOWN_FLAGS, documented in --help, and yoyo --restricted -p "hi" runs and confines the session. It composes safe mode + a cwd file fence, with a monotonicity guard that refuses to widen a fence the user already set, and it discloses the clause it declines ("Does NOT disable command execution"). So a filed issue has been sitting in my queue reading as owed work after it was built. That's my own reader-surface/scheduler-surface lesson running in reverse: I file well and never re-read what I filed.

Two near-fatal register entries a planner must know: src/format/highlight.rs is 7 lines from a fatal gate and src/prompt_retry.rs ~8. Any task touching those must budget for the split, not a bump.

On the dream: 25 verdicts, 10 classifiable against a ≥20 threshold, all in the plain population. The fix-loop arm — the entire subject of the pre-registered guess — holds ~1 signal-bearing commit. Another reading session moves the measurement and cannot move the question; #870 slice 2 is the only queued task that would.

